WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.695
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.695 --> 00:00:06.200
I help some IoT devices find
their long lost friends.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.752
Is friendship the best
configuration for performance?

00:00:08.752 --> 00:00:09.710
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:13.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:15.410
One of the big challenges
with IoT devices

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:17.210
is that there's a
tendency for each device

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:18.800
to use a different
set of protocols

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.290
for communicating with
other devices around them.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:23.780
This is where IOT
exchange comes in.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:27.710
Their product is somewhat like
a dating app for IoT devices,

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:30.590
where a device can find other
local devices that communicate

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:32.090
on similar protocols.

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.370
Their system was running
on a federation of VMs

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:37.400
inside of Google Cloud
using persistent disks

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.470
to host relational databases
that they were using

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:41.060
to keep everything in sync.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.710
Sadly, after some
profiling, they

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:45.251
realized they weren't getting
the performance they wanted out

00:00:45.251 --> 00:00:46.290
of the disk itself.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.230
Now, if you remember,
persistent disks

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:49.850
are the ones that
aren't physically

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:51.500
attached to your
host VM hardware,

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:52.880
and they come in two flavors--

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:56.750
standard and SSD, that each
one has their own performance

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:59.390
characteristics, which,
unsurprisingly, matters a lot

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:01.280
for your usage configuration.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.590
Now, for persistent
disks in general,

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:07.130
performance scales as a function
of the size of the disk.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:10.010
The bigger it is,
the faster it is.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:13.070
We can see here that for a
standard persistent disk,

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:15.890
the IOPS and throughput
scale pretty well in relation

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:17.210
to the size of the disk.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:20.660
SSD persistent disks
show the same behavior,

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.650
although the numbers are
significantly higher.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.100
However, SSD
persistent disks also

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.190
have performance scale
with the number of the CPUs

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:31.790
that the VM has, or
as the [? docs ?] say,

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.910
"SSD persistent disk
performance scales linearly

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.490
until it reaches either
the limits of the volume

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:40.070
or the limits of each
Compute Engine instance."

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:40.880
Hmm.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.190
Which was where the
IoT exchange folks

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:44.400
were running into trouble.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.240
Their VMs didn't have
enough CPUs for the IOPS

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:50.060
they needed to their SSD PDs.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:52.640
By moving them from 8
core to 32 core machines,

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.490
they saw their IOP
performance almost double.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.532
You got to love it when
data saves the day.

00:01:57.532 --> 00:01:59.490
If you'd like to know
more about today's topic,

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:02.100
check out the article listed
in the description below.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.224
And don't forget to check
out the rest of our Cloud

00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:05.780
Performance Atlas
content on YouTube,

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.321
because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:07.321 --> 00:02:08.450
every millisecond counts.

